Subject: [PATCH 5/5] fish: leave a terminal's answers out of the line and the keys

The reader decodes the strings and reports a terminal sends in answer to a query, and keeps the
ones it uses. Two kinds of answer it does not use were not read to their end.

The terminal's tertiary device attributes (`ESC P ! | id ST`) and its report of a setting
(`ESC P 1 $ r setting ST`) are device control strings. The reader gave up on them after their first
byte or two: the rest was left in the input, and the reader typed it into the line. Once the header
says which string it is, the string is now read to its terminator, as the strings the reader does
use already are. A byte that does not come within the reader's own wait for the next byte of a
sequence ends the string early, as it ends any other. A device control string whose header is
anything else is left as it was, so text typed behind an Alt-Shift-p is not taken for one.

A control sequence that ends in `t` was always taken for the old mouse-highlight report and took the
next two bytes with it. A report of the window (`CSI 8 ; rows ; columns t`) is such a sequence, and
the two bytes behind it, the person's next keys, were lost. The mouse report has no parameters, so
a sequence that has some is no longer taken for one.

This patch is a derivative work of fish and carries the GNU General Public Licence, version 2; see
shells/fish/LICENSE.

--- a/src/input/decode.rs
+++ b/src/input/decode.rs
@@ -398,6 +398,12 @@
                 return Some(CharEvent::Implicit(ImplicitEvent::MouseLeft(position)));
             }
             b't' => {
+                if count != 0 || params[0][0] != 0 {
+                    // A sequence with parameters is a report of the window (`CSI 8 ; rows ; columns
+                    // t`, which a terminal sends in answer to a query), and no mouse report has any:
+                    // the two characters behind it are the person's.
+                    return None;
+                }
                 flog!(reader, "mouse event");
                 // VT200 button released in mouse highlighting mode at valid text location. 5 chars.
                 let _ = next_char(self);
@@ -698,9 +704,28 @@
                 self.parse_xtversion(buffer);
                 return None;
             }
+            b'!' => {
+                // The terminal's tertiary device attributes (`\e P ! | id ST`) are a string this
+                // reader does not use. Its body is read to the string terminator, so none of it is
+                // left to be typed into the line. Only the header says it is one: Alt-Shift-p,
+                // typed before text, is not.
+                if self.read_sequence_byte(buffer)? == b'|' {
+                    self.read_until_sequence_terminator(buffer, false);
+                }
+                return None;
+            }
             _ => return None,
         };
-        if self.read_sequence_byte(buffer)? != b'+' {
+        let intermediate = self.read_sequence_byte(buffer)?;
+        if intermediate == b'$' {
+            // The terminal's report of a setting (`\e P 1 $ r setting ST`) is another, read the
+            // same way once its header is whole.
+            if self.read_sequence_byte(buffer)? == b'r' {
+                self.read_until_sequence_terminator(buffer, false);
+            }
+            return None;
+        }
+        if intermediate != b'+' {
             return None;
         }
         if self.read_sequence_byte(buffer)? != b'r' {
